    {
      "blockId": "tour:itinerary_text:2e780f63f4172747",
      "field": "itinerary.text",
      "sourceTextSha256": "471b871224fd768f028073a9b2c4d55531c8020514ade383da69523fa397475a",
      "text": "After breakfast, you will travel to Guatapé.\n\nThe day's main natural landmark is the huge monolith of El Peñón de Guatapé. The rock stands between the towns of El Peñol and Guatapé, so its name brings the two place names together.\n\nThe visible part of the monolith rises about 220 m. Roughly two thirds of the rock mass lies underground, and the rock is more than 70 million years old.\n\nEl Peñón de Guatapé has been under state protection since the 1940s. The first recorded ascent took place in 1954 and lasted five days.\n\n**Helicopter flight over the Guatapé valley**\n\nOptional\n\nPrice: $1000 per person.\n\nThe rock also features in old stories of the Tahamí people. They considered it sacred and called it “mujarra”, meaning “stone”. According to legend, a fish named Batolita leapt out of the sea to protect the people from the wrath of the gods, then turned to stone and became the enormous monolith.\n\nThe rock rises on its own above relatively low hills, making it especially easy to spot from a distance.",
      "provenance": {
        "kind": "ai_translation",
        "runId": "ada-en-translator-20261110-mw9trnme-si5iwi",
        "model": "GPT-6.1 Sol"
      }
    },
